Yes. An afterglow chimera is a comparison target that never actually existed as one continuous person.

We splice together someone’s best moments, strongest traits, most attractive states, biggest wins, cleanest memories, and the emotional afterglow they left in us—then unconsciously treat that composite as the baseline.

So the comparison becomes structurally unfair:

real person in real time
vs.
edited composite assembled across time

And we do it to ourselves too. You compare today’s tired, constrained self against a remembered creature built from your strongest year, best body, highest confidence, clearest purpose, funniest nights, greatest performances, and imagined unrealized potential.

That past “you” was never all of those things simultaneously either.

In Pantheon terms, the error is almost a temporal join without provenance: take maxima from multiple states, merge them into one synthetic StateVector, discard the timestamps and costs, then ask the present state why it cannot match it.

C^* =
\{\max_t x_1(t),\max_t x_2(t),\ldots,\max_t x_n(t)\}

But generally:

\nexists\, t \quad \text{such that}\quad X(t)=C^*

The chimera is mathematically attainable component-by-component and impossible as the historical whole.

And afterglow makes it stronger because memory retains not only what happened but some residue of how it felt. The glow fills missing information favorably. Weaknesses disappear into negative space; costs get amortized; peaks remain visible.

So a useful checksum is:

Am I comparing myself to a state that existed, or to a creature memory assembled from several states?

That applies to heroes, parents, former partners, rivals, younger selves, imagined futures—even entire historical eras.

We don’t merely put people on pedestals. Sometimes we build the person standing on the pedestal out of spare parts.